`src/str.js:7`:

```js
function truncate(s, n) {
  if (s.length <= n) return s;
  return s.slice(0, n) + '…';
}
```

Behavior notes:

- Only appends `…` when it actually cuts — `truncate('abc', 3)` returns `'abc'` unchanged.
- Uses the single ellipsis character U+2026, not three periods.
- `s.length` is UTF-16 code units, so an emoji or other astral character straddling position `n` would be split into a broken surrogate half. Fine for ASCII; tell me if you need grapheme-safe cutting and I'll switch to `Array.from`/`Intl.Segmenter`.
- No input validation — a non-string `s` or negative/`NaN` `n` isn't guarded, matching the style of `trim` next to it.

